    <description>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "blocks": {
    "contentData": [],
    "settingsData": [],
    "expose": [],
    "Layout": {}
  },
  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EProposals \u003Cstrong\u003Emust \u003C/strong\u003Eshow how minimum separation distances set out in Lists 4 and 5 are accommodated. This is to protect residential amenity, privacy, ensure minimum garden depth and prevent overbearing and terracing effects. A layout plan \u003Cstrong\u003Emust \u003C/strong\u003Ebe provided clearly showing the location of main, secondary and site aspect windows: \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u2022 Main Aspect windows: are main windows to living and dining rooms. All windows in through lounge/dining areas will be classed as main windows if they are diametrically opposite each other. In dining kitchens, the window lighting the dining area will be classed as the main window \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u2022 Secondary Aspect windows: are bedrooms and secondary windows to living/dining rooms and kitchens \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u2022 Side Aspect windows: are windows to non-habitable rooms (such as halls, utility rooms) and blank walls \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EList 4 sets out the minimum separation distances for 2 storey dwellings or between dwellings and boundaries. List 5 sets out the minimum separation distances 2 storey dwellings depending on what aspect windows the wall contains. These distances help ensure good privacy, daylight and access for maintenance. For proposals above 2 storeys, additional distance or stepping back of upper floors may be required to maintain privacy and amenity, with the intended approach to be agreed with the Council.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EException:\u003C/strong\u003E A reduction in the recommended separation distances may be acceptable to achieve higher densities Suburban Area Type (40\u002Bdph), Urban Area Type (50\u002Bdph) and Highly Urban Area Type (80\u002Bdph)) or to accommodate site constraints. Proposals \u003Cstrong\u003Emust \u003C/strong\u003Edemonstrate that residential amenity is maintained, specifically in relation to privacy for habitable rooms, daylight, overshadowing and microclimate (see code C2.19 and guidance G5.12).\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </description>
